# The highest mobile build number that has ever reached a store.
#
# BUILD_NUMBER is github.run_number, counted per workflow FILE. Renaming
# mobile-release.yml restarts it at 1, and every upload after that is
# refused as a duplicate -- recoverable only by burning version numbers
# until the count climbs back past this line. Preflight compares against
# this file so that failure happens in the first job with the cause
# named, rather than at the store with a message that says only that the
# number has been seen before.
#
# Why a committed file rather than the previous release's assets: this
# file does not depend on any job downstream of the upload, so it records
# what shipped even when a later job fails.
#
# The note here used to say `publish` had never run because
# android-release fails Preflight without the #673 secrets. That is no
# longer true: android-release is SKIPPED rather than failed, so publish
# does run, and runs 26, 27 and 28 each completed it. The file is still
# the right record -- it is written before the upload rather than derived
# from it -- but not for the reason previously given.
#
# Raise it when a build reaches TestFlight or Play. Non-contiguous is
# correct: a rejected upload still consumes its number.
#
# You no longer have to REMEMBER to raise it: the Mobile release run's
# summary names the value as a required follow-up, and
# scripts/check-mobile-build-mark.py compares this line with the `build<N>`
# of the newest published mobile asset. Per commit (CI's lint job) a lag
# is a warning naming the value to write; the next mobile release's
# Preflight refuses to build until it is fixed (#1418). It is
# a comparison against the assets, not a replacement for this file --
# this file is written before the upload, so it still records a number
# consumed by a run whose `publish` never finished, which no asset can.
#
# Why a guard was needed when Preflight already reads this file: Preflight
# only catches a DECREASE. BUILD_NUMBER climbs every run, so a mark
# lagging by three still passes `BUILD_NUMBER > HIGH` -- run 29 cleared
# 25 as easily as 28. Staleness was invisible to the only reader, which
# is how it survived six chore PRs (#714, #731, #740, #768, #786, #867)
# without one release failing. The cost was never a blocked release; it
# was this file naming the wrong number to whoever is debugging a
# rejected upload. See #787.
35
